{"name":"Light Time Travel","hex":"#c5d2df","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"time","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inevitable progression into the future with the passing of present and past events.","The feeling of the passage of events and their relative duration, as experienced by an individual."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To measure or record the time, duration, or rate of something.","To choose when something commences or its duration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/time"},{"term":"travel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be on a journey, often for pleasure or business and with luggage; to go from one place to another.","To pass from one place to another; to move or transmit."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of traveling; passage from place to place.","A series of journeys."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/travel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Time travel","blocks":[{"text":"Time travel is the hypothetical activity of traveling into the past or future. Time travel is a concept in philosophy, space, time and fiction, particularly science fiction. In fiction, time travel is typically achieved through the use of a device known as a time machine. The idea of a time machine was popularized by H. G. Wells's 1895 novel The Time Machine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/330px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_travel"},{"title":"Time","blocks":[{"text":"Time is the continuous progression of existence that occurs in an apparently irreversible succession from the past, through the present, and into the future. Time dictates all forms of action, age, and causality, being a component quantity of various measurements used to sequence events, to compare the duration of events or the intervals between them, and to quantify rates of change of quantities in material reality or in conscious experience. Time is often referred to as the fourth dimension and the temporal dimension, in addition to the three spatial dimensions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/330px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time"},{"title":"Travel","blocks":[{"text":"Travel is the movement of people between distant geographical locations. Travel can be done by foot, bicycle, automobile, train, boat, bus, airplane, ship or other means; with or without luggage; and can be one way or round trip. Travel can also include relatively short stays between successive movements, as in the case of tourism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Cross_Harbour_Tunnel_%281%29.JPG/330px-Cross_Harbour_Tunnel_%281%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Travel"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/500px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/1280px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/500px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first page of The Time Machine published by Heinemann","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Irvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg/250px-Irvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Irvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Irvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg/250px-Irvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Rip Van Winkle in Irvington, New York","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIrvington_statue_of_Rip_van_Winkle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/A_Christmas_Carol_-_Mr._Fezziwig%27s_Ball.jpg/500px-A_Christmas_Carol_-_Mr._Fezziwig%27s_Ball.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/A_Christmas_Carol_-_Mr._Fezziwig%27s_Ball.jpg/500px-A_Christmas_Carol_-_Mr._Fezziwig%27s_Ball.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig dance in a vision shown to Scrooge by the Ghost of Christmas Past.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Christmas_Carol_-_Mr._Fezziwig's_Ball.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/WelcomeKrononauts_Artforum_Jan1980_p.90_800x600.png/500px-WelcomeKrononauts_Artforum_Jan1980_p.90_800x600.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/WelcomeKrononauts_Artforum_Jan1980_p.90_800x600.png/500px-WelcomeKrononauts_Artforum_Jan1980_p.90_800x600.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advertisement placed in a 1980 edition of Artforum, advertising the Krononauts event","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWelcomeKrononauts_Artforum_Jan1980_p.90_800x600.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Time_dilation02.gif/250px-Time_dilation02.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Time_dilation02.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Time_dilation02.gif/250px-Time_dilation02.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Transversal time dilation. The blue dots represent a pulse of light. Each pair of dots with light \"bouncing\" between them is a clock. For each group of clocks, the other group appears to be ticking more slowly, because the moving clock's light pulse has to travel a larger distance than the stationary clock's light pulse. That is so, even though the clocks are identical and their relative motion is perfectly reciprocal.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATime_dilation02.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/500px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/500px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt"